    {
      "question": "e-21112",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "fff74e8a64472aff405019b6f9a098ff0622b7155329bd379035288f29a7deba",
      "new_sha256": "5b9aeaa8e384ee583f6d2071deeef23c319467320968fc115a30ba7e80808e10",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-21112",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Since $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\nc\\mathbf{u}(t) + d\\mathbf{v}(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for all $c,d\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w}(t)=4\\mathbf{u}(t)+6\\mathbf{v}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t).$ So, in order to find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ we just need to find $\\mathbf{w}'(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(4{e}^{4t})^{\u2032} \\\\ (6{e}^{5t})^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16{e}^{4t} \\\\ 30{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/example-21112.json",
          "file_sha256": "2464c18ba651749652297c39a75d44784a02237097f8189e1ff93c25a6e0281e"
        }
      ],
